#{[17592186121062 "To add two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we just need to add their corresponding components. To illustrate, consider the vectors $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^4,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ - 3 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad \\quad \\mathbf{b}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b},$ all we have to do is to add each component of the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ with the corresponding component of $\\mathbf{b}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5+(-5) \\\\ 1+3 \\\\ -3+1 \\\\ 6+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can only compute the sum, difference, or dot product of two vectors if both vectors have the same number of components. For example, we can't find the following sum, so we say it's *undefined*:\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]} +  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]} = {\\color{red}\\times} \\:(\\textrm{undefined})\n$$"] [17592186121063 "Let's again consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nTo find an expression for the null space using set-builder notation, we need to find all the solutions of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Let's start by considering the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\, \\mathbf{0}]$ and finding its reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,0] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st column). The second column is not a pivot column.\n\nNow, we convert the reduced matrix back into a system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, $\\color{blue}x_1$ is a basic variable (it corresponds to the pivot column), while $\\color{red}x_2$ is a free variable (it corresponds to the non-pivot column). Because there is a free variable, the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions. \n\nFrom the first equation we get ${\\color{blue}x_1}=-3{\\color{red}x_2},$ so the set of all the solutions of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A) = \n\t\\left\\{  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 3 {\\color{red}{x}_{2}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{x}_{2}}\\end{array} \\right]} \\: : \\: {\\color{red}x_2} \\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It is not necessary to consider the full augmented matrix $[A \\,|\\, \\mathbf{0}]$ since the last column won't be changed during the process of reduction. Really, we just need to find the reduced row echelon form of the matrix $A$ and then set up the corresponding system."] [17592186135045 "A set of vectors ${U}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is an **orthogonal set** if each pair of distinct vectors from the set is orthogonal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following set:\n\n$$\n{W}=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ - 4 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nIf we denote the vectors in our set ${W}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{w}_1, \\mathbf{w}_2, \\mathbf{w}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${W}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's compute each dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo indeed, ${W}$ is an orthogonal set.\n\nIf an orthogonal set is also a basis, then we call it an **orthogonal basis**. Here, $W$ is an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ because it contains three linearly independent (you can check this) orthogonal vectors."] [17592186135090 "A set of vectors ${U}=\\{\\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}$ is an **orthonormal set** if it is an orthogonal set of *unit* vectors. Remember that $\\mathbf u_i$ is a unit vector if $\\Vert \\mathbf u_i \\Vert = 1,$ or equivalently, $\\mathbf u_i \\cdot \\mathbf u_i = 1.$\n\nAn orthonormal set that is also a basis, like the standard basis, is called an **orthonormal basis**. For instance, the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{e}_1, \\mathbf{e}_2, \\mathbf{e}_3\\}$ of $\\mathbb R^3$ is an orthonormal basis because the vectors are orthogonal to each other and each vector in the set is a unit vector.\n\nThere are two more things to note about orthogonal sets:\n\n- Any orthogonal set of non-zero vectors is linearly independent.\n\n- An orthogonal set in $\\Bbb R^n$ cannot contain more than $n$ vectors. More precisely, if $V = \\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\dots, \\mathbf{v}_p\\}$ is an orthogonal set of non-zero vectors and $\\mathbf{v}_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^n,$ then we must have that $p\\leq n.$\n\n**Note:** To remember that an orthogonal set of non-zero vectors in $\\Bbb R^n$ cannot contain more than $n$ vectors, you can use the following intuitive mnemonic. There are only $n$ dimensions in $\\Bbb R^n,$ and each non-zero orthogonal vector has a $1$-dimensional span, meaning that it \"takes up\" $1$ dimension of $\\Bbb R^n.$ So, you can't \"fit\" more than $n$ orthogonal vectors in $\\Bbb R^n.$"] [17592186153353 "In the previous example, we computed a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ where \n\n$$\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 2 & - 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe basis that we found previously was\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} = \\Bigg\\{ \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_1}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_2} \n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Col}(A) = \\textrm{Span}\\,\\mathcal B\n= \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf a_1, \\mathbf a_2\\}\n\n= \n\\textrm{Span} \\Bigg\\{ \n\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}_3$ was not included in the basis because the $3$rd column of $A$ was not a pivot column. This means that $\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\nNow, what if we want to find the exact linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that yields $\\mathbf{a}_3?$ That is to say, we want to find $x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}x_1} \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_1} + {\\color{rblack}x_2} \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_2} = \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_3}.\n$$\n\nThis is equivalent to a system with the augmented matrix\n$$\n\\left[\n\t{\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & - 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{array}}\n\t\\right].\n$$\n\nIf we reduced this matrix to the *reduced* row echelon form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t{\\begin{array}{cc|c}\\bbox[3pt,lightgray]{ {\\color{blue}1}} & 0 & {\\color{red}3} \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[3pt,lightgray]{ {\\color{blue}1}} & {\\color{red}- 2}\\end{array}}\n\t\\right].\n$$\n\nFinally, the third column of the *reduced* matrix tells us that $x_1=3$ and $x_2=-2,$ meaning that\n$$\n\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_3} = {\\color{red}3}\\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_1} + ({\\color{red}-2})\\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}.\n$$\n\nIn general, if the $n$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_n$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. \n\n**Watch out!** The matrix must be written in *reduced* row echelon form (with $1$'s for pivots and $0$'s above and below the pivots), not just in a row echelon form."]}
